    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d537">

                <pb facs="0556" n="544" sameAs="frus:frus1941v01/pg544"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">561.311F1 Advisory Committee/1060: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the United Kingdom</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Winant</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">London</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1941-11-13T00:00:00+01:00">November 13,
                            1941—midnight</date>.<lb/>[Received November 13—8:20 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>5432. Your 4867, October 31, 11 a.m. The Foreign Office informed me that they had
                    taken note of your message and had instructed Salter<anchor n="9"
                        xml:id="d537-f1"/> accordingly. If you find difficulty in dealing with the
                    wheat situation, please notify me as I am certain that we can get consideration
                    and support of the position you outlined in your message.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Winant</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d537-f1" n="9" xml:id="d537-f1-content">
                <p>Sir Arthur Salter, Chief British Representative at the renewed wheat
                    discussions.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
